<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Raleigh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1552–<a href="/years/1618">1618</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sir Walter Raleigh, Walter Ralegh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Raleigh (c. 1552–1618) was an English courtier, soldier, explorer and writer, a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. He sponsored the failed Roanoke colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and in 1595 sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>. Governor of Jersey from 1600, he was imprisoned by James I in 1603, wrote a History of the World in the Tower, and was executed in 1618 after a second Orinoco expedition attacked a Spanish settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh.jpg" alt="Walter Raleigh" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Walter_Ralegh_by_%27H%27_monogrammist.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
